+      "name": "What is the ADA?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "The Americans with Disabilities Act is the federal civil rights law that requires equal access in employment, public services, public accommodations, and telecommunications, so disabled people can take part on equal terms."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "What is an ADA Coordinator, and what does ADAC mean?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "ADAC is the Certified ADA Coordinator credential. It marks a coordinator trained to help an organization understand its ADA duties, coordinate access work across teams, and keep that work documented and moving. An ADA Coordinator is the person who carries that coordinating role inside an organization. Matthew Cicanese, ADAC, brings three decades of lived experience to the work alongside the credential: the eye came first, and the credential formalized it."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "What are the key disability awareness days, and when are they?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Five moments a year already put disability on board, HR, and communications calendars. April is Autism Acceptance Month. May is Mental Health Awareness Month, and it includes Global Accessibility Awareness Day. July is Disability Pride Month, with the ADA Anniversary on July 26. October is National Disability Employment Awareness Month, and it includes Invisible Disabilities Week. December 3 is the International Day of Persons with Disabilities. Awareness dates do not create the need. They create the deadline, so planning starts a quarter ahead. See the key dates and planning calendar."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
